{"language": "en", "duration": 90.7, "text": "Which price looks lower? $4 or $3.99? Obviously $3.99. But look at the gap! A single cent! One cent! Yet it's enough to change how your brain reads the price. This is called the left digit effect. We read prices from left to right, and the first digit weighs too much. Faced with $3.99, the brain remembers $3 and change. Faced with $4, it remembers $4. It feels a gap far bigger than that one cent. Researchers, including Manoj Thomas and Vicky Morwitz, have studied this bias. When the left digit drops a notch, people underestimate the real difference. Retailers know it. $9.99, $19.99, $49.99. A price just under the threshold, from coffee to couches. And the trap goes further. We filed a product under the left digit, under $10, under $50. We no longer compare to our budget, we compare to a threshold. So how do you defend yourself? First, always round up in your head. $9.99 is $10. Then ask yourself, would I buy it at $10? If the answer is no, it's not the product that attracts you, it's the number. Finally, look at the price per ounce or per unit, not the displayed price, and at what the purchase represents in your month. A price isn't just a number, it's a message to your brain. And you, which price ending in $9 has already made you buy? Tell us in the comments.", "words": [{"word": "Which", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.48}, {"word": "price", "start": 0.48, "end": 0.8}, {"word": "looks", "start": 0.8, "end": 1.14}, {"word": "lower:", "start": 1.14, "end": 1.46}, {"word": "four", "start": 1.96, "end": 2.1}, {"word": "dollars,", "start": 2.1, "end": 2.24}, {"word": "or", "start": 2.24, "end": 2.94}, {"word": "three", "start": 2.94, "end": 3.28}, {"word": "ninety-nine?", "start": 3.28, "end": 3.7}, {"word": "Obviously,", "start": 6.68, "end": 7.28}, {"word": "three", "start": 7.28, "end": 7.88}, {"word": "ninety-nine.", "start": 7.88, "end": 8.32}, {"word": "But", "start": 8.76, "end": 9.0}, {"word": "look", "start": 9.0, "end": 9.